import{H as e,at as t,it as n,vt as r}from"./chunks/framework.dmHZ7NHG.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"The points-of-sail domain","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"params":{"slug":"sailing","address":"654f2354-9530-8be5-949a-67c74dc339aa","receipt":"862f682c-3b53-83c3-972c-dcfc99eecf0f"},"relativePath":"docs/publications/sailing.md","filePath":"docs/publications/[slug].md"}`),a={name:`docs/publications/sailing.md`};function o(e,i,a,o,s,c){return r(),n(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="the-points-of-sail-domain" tabindex="-1">The points-of-sail domain <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-points-of-sail-domain" aria-label="Permalink to “The points-of-sail domain”">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>sailing as decidable arithmetic, demarcated — the ~45° no-go zone (45+45=90), points of sail on multiples of 45°, the close-hauled 3-4-5 beating triangle at a distance penalty (5&gt;3), apparent wind exceeding true (5&gt;4), a balanced helm as moment equilibrium (8·3=6·4) so the boat holds course and the captain rests, two tacks cancelling leeway, and precise tacks compounding linearly — sailing geometry and balance, not aero/hydrodynamics</p></blockquote><p>These 8 facts hold by decision. Each was proven in Lean 4 — no Mathlib, checked sorry-free — and every sentence below links the proof that seals it. This note claims nothing beyond what its theorems settle: not that the domain is complete, only that the prose says nothing the proofs do not. It was written by reading the sealed ledger; read the proofs, not the prose.</p><h2 id="the-facts-each-backed-by-its-proof" tabindex="-1">The facts, each backed by its proof <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-facts-each-backed-by-its-proof" aria-label="Permalink to “The facts, each backed by its proof”">​</a></h2><ul><li>A boat cannot sail directly into the wind: the no-go zone is about 45° either side, a 90° cone (45 + 45 = 90) where the sails luff and make no power. To go upwind you must sail around it, not through it. — <a href="/theorem/no_go_zone">proof</a>.</li><li>The points of sail fall on multiples of 45°: close-hauled ~45°, beam reach 90°, broad reach 135°, running 180° — each divisible by 45, and 180/45 = 4 quarters of the turn from the wind to dead downwind. — <a href="/theorem/points_of_sail">proof</a>.</li><li>Beating close-hauled makes good distance upwind along a right triangle: sailing 5 units at the close-hauled angle advances 3 toward the mark and 4 across — 3² + 4² = 5². Velocity made good is the upwind leg of that triangle. — <a href="/theorem/beating_sailing_triangle">proof</a>.</li><li>Sailing upwind costs distance: to make good 3 units toward the wind you sail 5 through the water (the 3-4-5 close-hauled leg), and 5 &gt; 3. Beating is always longer than the straight line you cannot take. — <a href="/theorem/beating_distance_penalty">proof</a>.</li><li>Apparent wind is the vector sum of the true wind and the boat’s own motion, so close-hauled it exceeds the true wind: a true wind of 4 with the boat making 3 across gives an apparent 5 — 5 &gt; 4. The faster you sail upwind, the more wind you feel. — <a href="/theorem/apparent_wind_exceeds_true">proof</a>.</li><li>When conditions are perfect the boat sails itself: a balanced helm is a moment equilibrium — the sail’s turning moment equals the keel’s (8·3 = 6·4 = 24) — so she holds her course with the tiller free. The captain rests; the balance steers. — <a href="/theorem/balanced_helm_holds_course">proof</a>.</li><li>Tacking zigzags to windward, and two equal tacks cancel the cross-wind drift: 4 units to port plus 4 to starboard net zero across (4 + (−4) = 0), leaving only the gain upwind. Symmetry erases the leeway. — <a href="/theorem/tacking_cancels_leeway">proof</a>.</li><li>Precisely executed orders compound linearly: each well-sailed tack gains the same 3 units upwind, so 1, 2, 3 tacks make good 3, 6, 9 — [1,2,3] → [3,6,9]. The magnitude of precision is that nothing is lost between the legs. — <a href="/theorem/precise_tacks_compound">proof</a>.</li></ul><h2 id="provenance" tabindex="-1">Provenance <a class="header-anchor" href="#provenance" aria-label="Permalink to “Provenance”">​</a></h2><p>These 8 proofs fold, order-invariant, to the receipt <code>862f682c-3b53-83c3-972c-dcfc99eecf0f</code> — recompute it from the same ledger and it returns. The note itself content-addresses to a uuid, so any edit is visible. Writing descends from reading: this note holds only what the proofs it links already sealed, and it was audited by uuidna&#39;s own honesty gate before it was published — a sentence that overreached a proof would have been refused, not shipped.</p><p>Source · <a href="https://github.com/uuidna/uuidna/blob/main/lean/Sailing.lean" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">lean/Sailing.lean</a> · <a href="/theorems">all theorems</a></p><hr><p><strong>Audited before published</strong> · this note passed uuidna&#39;s honesty gate at build time — every claim above links the proof that seals it. Content-address <code>654f2354</code> · proofs fold to receipt <code>862f682c</code> (the handles; the full uuids are in this page&#39;s meta, recomputable) · 8 sealed theorems.</p><p><a href="/publications">All publications</a> · <a href="/theorems">All theorems</a> · <a href="/trials">How auditing works</a></p>`,11)]])}var s=e(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};